{"This game ran to 94 moves, and was finished on Saturday morning [13 June].
Winter had an overpowering game in the first stages, but dissolved the
position too soon as it turned out. Morry, with rook, bishop and knight
against queen and bishop, eventually had winning chances through his
opponent's risky efforts to get a full point. At the final session, after 10
hours' play and 91 moves..."} 91... Nf4 92. Kh2 Rh4+ $2 ({BCM's contemporary
annotation:} 92... Bg1+ $1 93. Kh1 Bd4 $1 94. Kh2 Rh4+ (94... Kg5 $1 $19 {
Hiarcs}) 95. Kg3 Kg5 96. Qf1 (96. Bxf5 $1 $13 {Hiarcs}) 96... Nh5+ 97. Kg2 Rg4+
98. Kh3 (98. Kh2 Be5+ 99. Kh3 Rg3+) 98... Nf4+ 99. Kh2 Be5 100. Kh1 (100. Qc1
Kh5 101. Qd1 (101. Qd2 Rh4+ 102. Kg1 Bd4+ $1) 101... Nd3+) 100... Rh4+ 101. Kg1
Bd4+ {wins.}) 93. Kg3 Rg4+ 94. Qxg4 $1 {Source: BCM, July 1936, p312} 1/2-1/2

